<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<handout component="day29" xml:id="day-29-lecture">
    <title>Day 29</title>

    <introduction>
        <p>
            Ch 10.3: Convolutional Neural Nets
        </p>
    </introduction>

<objectives>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <p>Describe the architecture of a convolutional neural network.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>Identify the different types of layers, how they are arranged, and the purpose of each layer.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>Explain how convolution works.
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>Compute by hand the result of the convolution between a simple matrix and a simple filter.</p>
        </li>
      </ul>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>Explain what information convolutional neural networks capture through convolution.</p>
    </li>
  </ul>
</objectives>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)</title>
  <p>
    Last Time: Flattening the Image — Why Is It Not Great?
  </p>
  <sidebyside width="50%">
    <stack>
      <p>
        <md>
          \begin{pmatrix}
          1 &amp; 1 &amp; 0 \\
          4 &amp; 2 &amp; 1 \\
          0 &amp; 2 &amp; 1
          \end{pmatrix}
          \longrightarrow
          \begin{pmatrix}
          1 \\ 1 \\ 0 \\
          4 \\ 2 \\ 1 \\
          0 \\ 2 \\ 1
          \end{pmatrix}
        </md>
      </p>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul >
          <li><p>Just ignore the fact that we have a picture.</p></li>
          <li><p>Loses a lot of the structure that we might want for more complicated images.</p></li>
          <li><p>The answer is CNNs, which take in image data and build a neural network that specifically tracks that structure.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Example Data Set: CIFAR100 Data</title>
    <image source="figures/Fig10-5.png">
      <shortdescription>Examples of images from the CIFAR100 data set.</shortdescription>
    </image>


      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>60,000 images: 50,000 training and 10,000 test images.</p></li>
          <li><p>Labels with 20 superclasses (e.g., aquatic mammals).</p></li>
          <li><p>5 classes per superclass (beaver, dolphin, otter, seal, whale).</p></li>
          <li><p>Images are <m>32\times 32</m> pixels.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>


</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Image Channel Data</title>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Three 8-bit numbers per pixel (red, green, blue).</p></li>
          <li><p>Numbers are organized in a <m>feature map</m>, which is a three-dimensional array.</p></li>
          <li><p>The first two directions are spatial (<m>32\times 32</m>).</p></li>
          <li><p>The third is the <m>channel</m> axis, representing the three colors.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <image source="figures/Fig10-8-onlychannels.png">
        <shortdescription>Illustration of the three color channels of an image: red, green, and blue.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>How Does Your Brain Do It? The Visual Hierarchy</title>
  <image source="figures/Visual_Hierarchy.jpeg">
    <shortdescription>Illustration of the visual hierarchy in human visual processing.</shortdescription>
  </image>
  <p>
    Mauro Manassi, Bilge Sayim, and Michael H. Herzog, “When crowding of crowding leads to uncrowding,” <i>Journal of Vision</i> 13(13):10 (2013).
    <url href="https://doi.org/10.1167/13.13.10">https://doi.org/10.1167/13.13.10</url>.
  </p>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>CNNs</title>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Really similar to the human visual hierarchy.</p></li>
          <li><p>Earlier layers identify low-level features in the input image, such as small edges and patches of color.</p></li>
          <li><p>Low-level features are combined into higher-level features (parts of ears, eyes, etc.) in later layers.</p></li>
          <li><p>This is done using special types of hidden layers: <m>convolution</m> layers and <m>pooling</m> layers.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <image source="figures/Fig10-6.png">
        <shortdescription>Diagram illustrating the hierarchical feature extraction process in a convolutional neural network.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Convolution Layer: Convolution Filter</title>
  <sidebyside widths="55% 40%">
      <p>Original Image:
        <md>
          \begin{bmatrix}
          a &amp; b &amp; c\\
          d &amp; e &amp; f\\
          g &amp; h &amp; i\\
          j &amp; k &amp; l
          \end{bmatrix}
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>Convolution Filter:
        <md>
          \begin{bmatrix}
          \alpha &amp; \beta\\
          \gamma &amp; \delta
          \end{bmatrix}
        </md>
      </p>
  </sidebyside>
        <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Note that we are just doing an inner product here, which measures (approximately) similarity.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
        <p>Convolved Image
      <md>
\begin{bmatrix}
a\alpha + b\beta + d\gamma + e\delta \amp b\alpha + c\beta + e\gamma + f\delta \\
d\alpha + e\beta + g\gamma + h\delta \amp e\alpha + f\beta + h\gamma + i\delta \\
g\alpha + h\beta + j\gamma + k\delta \amp h\alpha + i\beta + k\gamma + l\delta
\end{bmatrix}
</md>
      </p>
</paragraphs>

<example>
  <title>Convolution Filter Example</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>Consider the image represented by the following matrix. For each task, apply the specified convolution filter to compute the convolved image.</p>
    <image source="figures/CNN-examplematrix.png" width="60%">
      <shortdescription>Example image represented as a matrix of pixel values.</shortdescription>
    </image>
  </introduction>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>Apply the following convolution filter:</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          \begin{bmatrix}
          0 &amp; 1\\
          0 &amp; 1
          \end{bmatrix}
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>What is the convolved image?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>The convolved image is</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          \begin{bmatrix}
          1 &amp; 3\\
          0 &amp; 3\\
          1 &amp; 3
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>The convolved image highlights regions that resemble the convolution filter. Notice that the result is smaller than the original image.</p>
    </solution>
  </task>
  <task>
    <statement>
      <p>Now apply a different convolution filter:</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          \begin{bmatrix}
          1 &amp; 1\\
          0 &amp; 0
          \end{bmatrix}
        </md>
      </p>
      <p>What is the convolved image?</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>The convolved image is</p>
      <p>
        <md>
          \begin{bmatrix}
          4 &amp; 3\\
          2 &amp; 1\\
          3 &amp; 2
          \end{bmatrix}.
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </task>
</example>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Convolution Filter: Bigger Example</title>
  <sidebyside widths="65% 35%">
    <stack>
      <image source="figures/Fig10-7.png">
        <shortdescription>Example of convolution filters applied to an image of a tiger, highlighting regions that resemble the filters.</shortdescription>
      </image>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>The original image is a <m>192\times 179</m> image of a tiger.</p></li>
          <li><p>Convolution filters are <m>15\times 15</m> images consisting mostly of zeros (black), with a narrow strip of ones (white).</p></li>
          <li><p>The filters highlight areas of the tiger that resemble the filter.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Convolution Layer</title>

      <image source="figures/Fig10-8.png" width="70%">
        <shortdescription>Diagram illustrating convolution filters applied to the three color channels of an image and combined into output feature maps.</shortdescription>
      </image>

      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>The image is in color, so it has three channels.</p></li>
          <li><p>This means the convolution filter will have three channels, one per color, each of dimension <m>3\times 3</m>.</p></li>
          <li><p>The results of the three convolutions are summed to form a two-dimensional output. Color is "forgotten" at this point.</p></li>
          <li><p>Using <m>K</m> different filters produces <m>K</m> two-dimensional output maps, which can then be treated as channels.</p></li>
          <li><p>Padding can be used to make the updated image the same size as the original.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>

</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>More Notes on Convolution</title>
  <p>
    <ul>
      <li><p>The idea of filters comes from image processing.</p></li>
      <li><p>The novelty in deep learning is that the choice of filters is learned.</p></li>
      <li><p>Filter weights: there is one hidden unit per pixel in the convolved image, but the weights are more constrained than in a fully connected network.</p></li>
      <li><p>The same weights are used for all patches of the image and across channels. Each filter has only <m>m\times m</m> weights, where <m>m</m> is the patch size.</p></li>
      <li><p>ReLU is applied to the convolved image. This is sometimes viewed as a separate layer in a CNN, called a detector layer.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </p>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Pooling Layers</title>

      <p>
        <ul >
          <li><p>Condense a large image into a smaller summary image.</p></li>
          <li><p>There are many options, but max pooling is common.</p></li>
          <li><p>For each non-overlapping <m>2\times 2</m> block of pixels, produce a single new pixel with the maximum value from that block.</p></li>
          <li><p>Provides location invariance: as long as a large value occurs in one of the four pixels, the whole block registers as having a large value.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
<p>
    The following is an illustration of max pooling, where each non-overlapping two-by-two block is replaced by its maximum value.
<md>
\text{Max pool}\quad
\begin{bmatrix}
1 \amp 2 \amp 5 \amp 3\\
3 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp 2\\
2 \amp 1 \amp 3 \amp 4\\
1 \amp 1 \amp 2 \amp 0
\end{bmatrix}
\longrightarrow
\begin{bmatrix}
3 \amp 5\\
2 \amp 4
\end{bmatrix}
</md>
</p>


</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>Putting It Together to Make a CNN</title>
  <image source="figures/Fig10-8.png">
    <shortdescription>Architecture of a convolutional neural network, showing convolution and pooling layers followed by fully connected layers and a softmax output.</shortdescription>
  </image>
  <sidebyside widths="50% 50%">
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Start with three channels.</p></li>
          <li><p>Get a new channel at the first hidden layer for each filter. Use padding to keep the same size.</p></li>
          <li><p>The next layer is a max pooling layer.</p></li>
          <li><p>Convolve and pool several more times.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <p>
        <ul >
          <li><p>Since the size of the image decreases, increase the number of filters in the next convolution layer to compensate.</p></li>
          <li><p>Flatten the result, then feed it into one or more fully connected layers.</p></li>
          <li><p>The final layer uses a softmax activation for the 100 classes.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
  <p>
    Check out the following tool for an interactive visualization of CNNs.
    <url href="https://poloclub.github.io/cnn-explainer/">CNN Explainer: Interactive Visualization</url>
  </p>
</paragraphs>

<paragraphs>
  <title>TL;DR</title>
  <sidebyside widths="48% 48%">
    <stack>
      <p>Feed-Forward Neural Network</p>
      <image source="figures/Fig10-4.png">
        <shortdescription>Architecture of a feed-forward neural network.</shortdescription>
      </image>
      <p>
<md>
<mrow> A_k \amp= h_k(\mathbf{X})</mrow>
<mrow> \amp= g\left(w_{k0} + \sum_{j=1}^{p} w_{kj}X_j\right)</mrow>
</md>
</p>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Combines input data using learned weights.</p></li>
          <li><p>Uses a linear combination of the resulting activations to obtain the output.</p></li>
          <li><p>Sometimes uses a softmax activation to obtain classification probabilities.</p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
    <stack>
      <p>Convolutional Neural Network (CNN)</p>
      <image source="figures/Fig10-8.png">
        <shortdescription>Architecture of a convolutional neural network.</shortdescription>
      </image>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>A specialized neural network designed to process image data.</p></li>
          <li>
            <p>Builds subsequent layers using:
            <ul>
              <li><p>Convolution layers</p></li>
              <li><p>Pooling layers</p></li>
              <li><p>Fully connected layers</p></li>
            </ul>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </stack>
  </sidebyside>
</paragraphs>

</handout>